ducing pest pressures and  mitigating negative effects that might  occur for beneficial organisms  especially pollinators  and there's some evidence that organic  soil management specifically can  condition soil in a way that improves  crop resistance to herbivory and  pathogens which is  interesting  because maybe we can  apply some of those practices that are  used in organic farming to non-organic  systems to at least reduce pesticide and  fungicide use um and some of the ways  that this happen can be by changing soil  nutrient profiles as well as um microbes  in the soil  and the changes to soil properties that  happen from soil conditioning due to  some of these management practices can  change leaves in a way that  is important to pest and pathogen  resistance so  leaf nutrient profiles can change due to  soil and so can some of the chemicals  that plants produce to defend themselves  against pathogens and insect pests  but we also know that the effects of  management practices can take time to  manifest so if we implement a certain  soil management practice in a single  year we might not actually see a  difference in something like pest  pressure or yield um or resistance to  pests i mean um  but  over time we might see a difference with  that management practice and that's  because of this soil conditioning that  can happen  to microbial community or soil structure  or maybe nutrient composition so one of  the focuses of my research when i think  about how soil affects  pest pressures is looking at how  long-term management decisions  over the course of several years can  affect  pest and pathogen resistance  but i'm also interested in how soil  management practices affect pollinators  as i said  there's a lot of discussion about  using cover crops for soil fertility and  how that might  dovetail with providing pollinator  habitat  and so if for example if we let cover  crops flower um we can create an  abundant resource pulse of nectar and  pollen for bees 